        "knowledge_point": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points",
        "problem": "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$",
        "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)",
